`,s.jsx(C,{items:[{q:"State precisely what p = 0.03 means, then state three things it does NOT mean.",a:"It means: if H0 were true, 3% of samples would produce a statistic at least this extreme. It does NOT mean there is a 3% chance H0 is true — H0 is not random and p was computed assuming it. It does NOT mean a 3% chance the result was due to chance. And it does NOT mean a 3% chance you are wrong to reject; that depends on how often H0 holds across the tests you run."},{q:"Why can't you reduce both alpha and beta at fixed n?",a:"They are two tails of the same picture. Shrinking alpha moves the rejection boundary further out, which necessarily puts more of the alternative distribution on the non-rejection side — raising beta. Only increasing n changes the picture, by shrinking the standard error so the two distributions overlap less."},{q:"A study reports p = 0.34 and concludes 'the drug has no effect.' What is wrong?",a:"Two things. It converts 'failed to reject' into 'accepted the null', which the test cannot support. And it ignores power: with a small sample or a noisy outcome, beta may be so high that a real, clinically important effect would usually go undetected. The honest statement is that this study did not detect an effect, plus the effect size it had power to find."},{q:"Why do particle physics and genomics use alpha far below 0.05?",a:"Because they run enormous numbers of tests. At alpha = 0.05, a million genomic comparisons yield 50,000 false positives by construction. Tightening alpha — 5-sigma in physics, Bonferroni-corrected thresholds in genomics — keeps the expected number of false discoveries near zero despite the multiplicity."},{q:"Show why the p-value and critical-value methods can never disagree.",a:"z* is DEFINED as the statistic whose tail area equals alpha. So |Z| = z* corresponds exactly to p = alpha, and because tail area decreases monotonically as the statistic grows, |Z| > z* is equivalent to p < alpha. They are the same inequality expressed on the statistic scale versus the probability scale."}]}),`
